-# Four-minute demo script
-
-Record the actual running app. All built-in examples are constructed. Do not present staged scenarios as real victim accounts or a benchmark. Keep private messages, personal information and unrelated desktop windows out of the recording.
-
-**0:00–0:25 — The decision.** “When an unexpected message asks us for money or a code, we need more than a confident label. TrustLens helps us pause, examine the evidence and choose a safer next step.” Add one genuine local observation here only after completing the community worksheet.
-
-**0:25–1:10 — Screenshot and review.** Select Screenshot and choose `static/demo-message.png`, a constructed screenshot included in this bundle. Read it, inspect the editable text, confirm the review and check it. Show the quoted request for a security code and specific advice. Explain that OCR can make mistakes and the text is not analyzed until reviewed. If Windows OCR is unavailable, paste the same example in Message and state that limitation.
-
-**1:10–1:40 — Avoiding false accusations.** In Message, check “Please share the source code for our school app.” Then check “Your one-time code is 123456. Do not share it with anyone.” Explain why neither is a request to disclose a security code. No matched warning is still not proof of safety. Copy a safe summary: it omits message text, names and contact details.
-
-Optional swap for this segment: use the visible **A link hiding in plain sight** practice check. Show the displayed bank address and different actual link host in the result. Explain that this difference is observed, but legitimate tracking may also produce it. Follow with **A normal safety reminder** to demonstrate that the checker can leave a message unflagged without declaring it safe.
-
-**1:40–2:10 — A concrete next step.** In Call, check “Move your savings to a safe account.” Show the independent callback and payment advice. Open the recovery guide → “I sent money.” Show the bank/payment-provider checklist and the FTC link. No account details are entered, no money is moved and recovery is not promised.
-
-**2:10–2:55 — Optional image and provenance.** Present component results only after a permitted-image check confirms their availability. Upload a permitted demonstration image and show the actual C2PA status and separate visual-model result. If either component is unavailable, show its actual unavailable/partial state. An unavailable visual model made no visual decision; unavailable C2PA means credentials could not be checked. Continue with Message, Call or Practice. Never substitute a historical score for a live result. Say: “No credentials is not proof of AI generation. A verified signature authenticates the signed statement, not the seller or transaction. Our image model can also be inconclusive.” Do not add test signing roots to the app or present an untrusted test signature as trusted. Native signature/tamper tests are documented separately.
-
-**2:55–3:25 — Learn a safer habit.** Open Practice. Use the bank-code question or the signed-photo transaction question. Show the explanation, not merely a score. If time permits, use Link's example to reveal the hostname after @ without visiting the destination.
-
-**3:25–4:00 — Evidence and limits.** Open How it works briefly. Explain local processing, lack of saved history, English rule limits, and the difference between development accuracy and independent release performance. Close with actual usability observations if collected; otherwise say the next step is testing with the proposed community. End with the running app and repository link.
-
-Preparation: launch once, use browser zoom 100%, close unrelated tabs, and record at a readable width. The portable source ZIP excludes large model weights and machine-specific launch configuration; optional image paths are not ready by default. If those components are configured, use a permitted image check to confirm availability and warm the model; otherwise use the fallback above. Do not imply the warm response represents cold startup speed.
-
